  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA cold front was analyzed from the Mid Atlantic region to the\nLower Ohio Valley region. A wave moving along the front could\nspread some showers across areas south of I-70 today. An\nisolated shower is possible across the remainder of the area,\nthough much of the time will be dry in easterly flow. \n\nThe front will return north as a warm front tonight, as a\ndigging shortwave trough and deepening surface low advance \nacross the Midwest. Showers are expected as the front lifts \nnorth, with isolated thunderstorms also possible with elevated \ninstability. A low level jet is also expected to increase and \ntrack across the Lower Great Lakes and Upper Ohio Valley region \ntonight. This should enhance lift, resulting in more numerous \nshowers and possible thunderstorms north of Pittsburgh. \n\nA portion of eastern Ohio was included in a Marginal Risk for \nsevere storms this evening. It appears there is a better chance \nfor severe storms upstream, and there is some question on how \nfar east this activity will reach before weakening. Overall, the\npotential for severe storms across the Upper Ohio Valley region\nappears limited, with the instability becoming elevated by the \ntime storms arrive. 0-6km shear is expected to range from \n45-55kt, which could result in a gusty wind potential until the \nstorms become elevated tonight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe warm front should lift north of the region Friday morning,\nas the deepening surface low tracks across Ontario. The low will\npull a strong cold front across the region from W-E from mid\nmorning into the early afternoon. With the slower FROPA timing,\nenough instability could build with some heating early in the \nday to result in a line of showers and embedded thunderstorms. \nThe Storm Prediction Center has included areas roughly east of \nI-79 in a Marginal Risk for severe storms/damaging wind gusts as\nthe front crosses. With a strong wind field in place aloft, any\nstronger shower or thunderstorm could mix this wind down to the\nsurface (wind at 10 kft is expected to be around 60 kt.) \n\nAfter FROPA, cold advection and a tight pressure gradient should\nresult in gusty wind from 30-40 mph across much of the area.\nProbabilities for advisory level gusts (greater than 45mph), and\ncurrently range from 5 percent to as much as 35 percent across\nthe lower elevations. It still appears the higher terrain areas\nwill see the highest gusts, with advisory level gusts the most\nlikely across eastern Tucker county WV later Friday into Friday\nnight. Overall, the less aggressive cold advection should result\nin slightly less steep lapse rates, which could limit gusts\noutside of the higher terrain. \n\nShowers are expected to increase again Friday night as the\nstrong upper trough crosses the region, and some lake\nenhancement occurs. Temperatures are currently expected to\nremain above freezing, with wind precluding a frost potential.\nWind should diminish on Saturday as high pressure builds in.\nClearing skies and diminishing wind should result in  \nwidespread frost and freeze conditions Saturday night into\nSunday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nA less amplified flow aloft is expected by next week as the deep\nupper trough exits the area. Rising 500mb height and surface\nhigh pressure should result in dry weather into the middle of\nnext week, with a gradual warming trend back to seasonable\ntemperatures.",
